What is a Kubernetes Sidecar Container? 🚀

✅A Pod can contain more than one container.
A Sidecar Container runs alongside the main application container to provide supporting functionality.
✍️For example:
Main container → Application
Sidecar container → Log forwarding or proxy functionality
Common use cases include:
✅ Log collection
✅ Service proxying
✅ Supporting agents
✅ Data synchronization
✅ Security-related functionality

Sidecars share the Pod's network environment and can share volumes when configured.

However, adding unnecessary sidecars can increase resource consumption and operational complexity.
Also, some sidecar functionality can now be implemented through native platform features or dedicated agents.
Use sidecars when they solve a clear problem—not simply because the pattern exists.
